        <p class="article__lead reveal">Uganda sits on the equator, so it's a year round destination, gorilla permits are issued in every month. What shifts with the seasons is how easy the forest trails are, where the game gathers, and how green and quiet the country feels. Here's how to choose.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Uganda's seasons at a glance</h2>
        <p class="reveal">There are two drier spells, <strong>June to September</strong> and <strong>December to February</strong>, and two wetter ones, the long rains (<strong>March to May</strong>) and the short rains (<strong>October to November</strong>). Being equatorial, Uganda is warm and green all year; even in the dry months a forest can deliver a sudden shower, so a light rain jacket is always worth packing.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best for gorilla &amp; chimp trekking</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The drier months, <strong>June to September</strong> and <strong>December to February</strong>, are ideal for trekking in <a href="bwindi.html">Bwindi</a> and <a href="kibale-forest.html">Kibale</a>, when the steep forest trails are firmest and least muddy. Permits are limited and sell out months ahead in these peak windows, so we book early. Trekking has a minimum age of 15.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best for game viewing</h2>
        <p class="reveal">For the savanna parks, <a href="queen-elizabeth.html">Queen Elizabeth</a> and <a href="murchison-falls.html">Murchison Falls</a>, the dry months concentrate wildlife around the rivers and channels and keep the tracks easy. June to August in particular offers reliable big game viewing alongside the boat safaris.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">The green seasons</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The wetter months (March to May and October to November) bring lush landscapes, dramatic skies, superb birding and noticeably fewer trekkers on the trail, and, often, lower lodge rates. Gorilla permits remain available, so flexible travellers who don't mind a muddier hike can have the forest almost to themselves.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">When to think twice</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The heart of the long rains (<strong>April to May</strong>) can make the forest trails genuinely demanding and some roads slow. For first time visitors set on a comfortable trek we usually steer toward the dry windows, but with good boots and the right spirit, the green season can be wonderful and rewarding.</p>

